  ],
  "text": "Loading a ship used to take days and a small army. A box cut it down to minutes. Before nineteen fifty six, cargo was sacks, barrels and crates, every one a different shape. Every item went on and came off by hand, and half the cost of shipping was that. Malcolm McLean's idea was not the box. It was that everybody use the same box. Same corners, same locks, same crane, in every port on Earth. Loading costs fell by well over ninety percent. Ports were rebuilt around cranes, and the old dockwork simply ended. But the real consequence was elsewhere. Shipping got cheap enough that location stopped mattering. A factory could sit on the other side of the planet and nobody would see it in the price. Global supply chains are not a policy. They are a consequence of the box. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
